{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"787cac3b-697a-4119-bcc4-f47c59acb2dd","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The color of the daytime sky is fundamentally the result of the interaction between sunlight and Earth's atmosphere. Light emitted by the Sun appears white to human eyes, but it is actually a composite of all the colors in the visible spectrum. Each of these colors travels in electromagnetic waves characterized by distinct wavelengths; red, orange, and yellow light travel in longer, lower-frequency wavelengths, while blue and violet light travel in significantly shorter, higher-frequency waves.\n\nWhen sunlight penetrates the atmosphere, it collides with gas molecules—primarily nitrogen and oxygen. Because these molecules are much smaller than the wavelengths of visible light, they scatter the incoming photons in all directions through a physical process known as Rayleigh scattering. This phenomenon is strongly wavelength-dependent: shorter wavelengths scatter far more efficiently than longer ones. Consequently, the shorter blue light waves are scattered repeatedly across every direction, filling the atmosphere with a luminous blue glow.\n\nAlthough violet light has an even shorter wavelength and scatters even more intensely than blue light, humans perceive the sky as blue rather than violet. This is due to a combination of solar output and human biology. The Sun emits a much higher intensity of blue light than violet light, and the photoreceptor cone cells in human eyes are far more sensitive to blue wavelengths than to violet ones. As a result, our visual system primarily registers the scattered blue light.\n\nסיכום: מולקולות הגז באטמוספירה מפזרות את אורכי הגל הקצרים של האור הכחול ביעילות רבה יותר משאר צבעי הקשת, ורגישות העין האנושית משלימה את המראה הכחול של השמיים.\n","duration_seconds":16.282435476,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":12293,"output_tokens":1358,"thinking_tokens":1015,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":13651}}}
